module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.duplicate_record_literal_field_silently_last_wins

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data duplicate_record_literal_field_silently_last_wins: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "duplicate_record_literal_field_silently_last_wins" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**one field of a record literal is assigned TWICE, the literal is accepted, and the LAST assignment silently wins** (INVALID STATE: a record literal names the same field key more than once with different values, and no phase objects. ",

    "HARM: the source states two values for one field and the machine picks one without saying so, so a reader cannot tell which value the program carries without knowing the resolution rule, and the author who believed the first one wrote a wrong answer that parses, typechecks, projects and passes every gate. ",

    "THE SPECIMEN IS LIVE ON MAIN AND IS NOT MINE (gunbc, 2026-09-04). `dag/gunbc/fleet/fleet_converge_plan_cli.dag` assigns `observed_unit_standings` twice inside ONE `FullHostConverge` literal -- once to `runner_unit_standings_unobserved_no_transaction` and once to `observe_runner_unit_standings_wet(host: host)`. Both lines were introduced by a SINGLE commit, 4b7371d1f64 (gunbc#10357), so this is not a merge artifact: one commit, one literal, one field, two assignments. That change MERGED, so every gate this repository owns passed a literal carrying two values for one field. ",

    "MEASURED ON A CONTROLLED FIXTURE, BOTH ARMS, at main 4b7371d1f64. `DupPair { a: 1, b: 3 }` answers 1. `DupPairSubject { a: 1, a: 2, b: 3 }` answers 2. The pair differs in exactly the fact at issue, so the observation is about the duplicate and not about an unrelated malformation. The ProcessExit-wrapped form of the subject exits SUCCESS on `p.a == 2`, so last-wins is read from a completed run rather than inferred. ",

    "**THE DUPLICATE REFUSED NOTHING, AND THIS SENTENCE IS HERE TO STOP THE ROW BEING MIS-CITED.** The first measurements were taken through a host-boundary refusal -- `function returned 2, not ProcessExit` -- which STATES the evaluated value. That refusal is the ProcessExit calling convention and has nothing to do with the duplicate. Nothing in the compiler objected to the repeated key at any point. A reader skimming for the word `error` in this row's evidence will find one that is not about the subject. ",

    "**THE CHECKER IS ALREADY THERE AND ENFORCES TWO OF THE THREE DIRECTIONS, which is what makes the ceiling concrete.** A record literal's field set can be wrong three ways: too few keys, an unknown key, or the same key twice. The first two REFUSE with located diagnostics. `MissPair { a: 1 }` on a two-field type refuses with `missing required field 'b' in literal of type 'MissPair'` at a line and column. An unknown key refuses too, measured not from a fixture but from main's own floor lane on 4b7371d1f64, which refused `field '__launch_excess_field_probe' not found in type 'LaunchEnvironmentConverge'`. THE DUPLICATE IS THE ONLY ONE OF THE THREE THAT IS SILENT. This is not a wall nobody built; it is one gap in a wall that already stands on both sides of it, held up by code that is already holding the field list at the moment the repeated key is read. ",

    "**THE ACCEPTED VERDICT IS BELIEVABLE ONLY BECAUSE OF THE CONTROL, and the near-miss is recorded because it produces the same observation as a passing wall.** The first run of BOTH arms failed identically with a workspace-root path panic, because source roots must live under the workspace root. Two identical failures on the subject and the control is exactly what `the compiler refuses duplicates` would also look like, so without the control this investigation would have closed a floor breach as good news. An instrument that cannot run and a wall that holds are indistinguishable from the subject arm alone. ",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: OUTSIDE THE LADDER, not the bottom rung of it. The state is writable, accepted and silent, and DESIGN section 4b places silent wrongness outside the ladder rather than on it, with section 5 forbidding it outright: picking one of two stated values without saying so is a fabricated plausible output. ",

    "CEILING: 3, structurally guaranteed, and the reason it is NOT 4 is the reason its sibling `exact_duplicate_match_arm_not_refused` gives for the same shape -- a record literal is written FIELD BY FIELD, so a second identical key is spellable in any source a human or a generator can emit, and no constructor arrangement removes it. The compiler can refuse every such program; it cannot make the state unwritable. Claiming 4 here would be rung inflation on a row whose whole subject is a floor breach. ",

    "**IT IS ONE OF A FAMILY OF THREE, AND NO MEMBER COVERS ANOTHER.** `exact_duplicate_match_arm_not_refused` is a duplicate ARM HEAD, accepted, source order deciding which body answers. `test.claim.duplicate_definition_binding_probe_test` measures a module DEFINING ONE NAME TWICE, accepted with zero diagnostics, second definition silently winning, and its own prose calls that below floor. This row is the duplicate FIELD KEY. One shape -- a repeated key in a keyed construct is accepted and resolves by source order -- across three constructs with three separate walls. They are filed separately rather than merged because the match-arm row states its grain narrowly and says why in terms: claiming the general form would put a ratchet where a wall is available, and would let a narrow wall be cited as coverage for the broad class. That prohibition binds this row too, in both directions. ",

    "**THE DISCRIMINATING RED IS A FIXTURE STRING AND NOT AN INLINE LITERAL, AND MAIN IS CURRENTLY DEMONSTRATING WHY.** An expecting-red written as a literal in a compiled module cannot flip when its wall lands: the wall makes the file UNPARSEABLE, so the probe breaks the corpus instead of going red, and the wall becomes unlandable over its own regression control. That is not a hypothetical. On 4b7371d1f64 the floor lane REFUSES -- not one witness failing, the whole phase refusing to resolve -- because `dag/test/claim/fleet/fleet_converge_launch_scope_non_interference_test.dag` writes `__launch_excess_field_probe` INLINE into a `LaunchEnvironmentConverge` literal. That probe was authored when the compiler ADMITTED an excess field; the field-set check has since reached it, and the probe did not flip, it took the lane down. DESIGN section 4b names the working shape: a state unrepresentable in the ACCEPTED corpus is still representable as SOURCE HANDED TO THE COMPILER BY A FIXTURE, and a compiler is precisely a thing whose regression probes are invalid programs. So this class's red lives in a source STRING, the corpus never contains the duplicate, and the assertion flips on the wall and on nothing else. ",

    "NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER: THE PARSER REFUSING A REPEATED FIELD KEY IN A RECORD LITERAL, with a located diagnostic naming the field and both positions. Nothing smaller retires this row. In particular, REPAIRING THE LIVE SPECIMEN DOES NOT: editing `fleet_converge_plan_cli.dag` removes the only authored instance while leaving the class fully alive, which is the move that converted `one red exists` into `no red exists` in the arity-zero case recorded in `test.claim.arity_zero_import_refusal_witness`. The specimen is evidence, not debt.)",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
